#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078230 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec980fcb-97ed-5c60-94de-0521d9d7df27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Continuous Function Given the Function Expression"}] :question/id #uuid "df884ed9-c75e-5a38-abe2-425e01ee4aa7" :question/math-academy-id "e-432" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^5 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\\,$?\n${3,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0 \\\\ 2x + 3,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 0$" :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-2}^{5}f(x)dx &= ∫_{-2}^{0}3dx + ∫_{0}^{5}(2x + 3)dx \\\\\n&= 3x \\mid _{-2}^{0} + (x^{2} + 3x) \\mid _{0}^{5} \\\\\n&= [3(0) - 3(-2)] + [(5^{2} + 3(5)) - (0^{2} + 3(0))] \\\\\n&= 6 + 25 + 15 \\\\\n&= 46\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078231 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "317454f0-3a04-5c1c-a451-2b8b2ec850ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-433" :question/problem "The graph of $y=f(x)$, shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/626/Source/Images/e969825468755643c99584897eed6f04.png)" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $[MATH: x \\in [0,2)]$ and the line segment for $x \\in [2,4]$. Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{-A_2}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $[MATH: x \\in [0,2).]$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n$A_{1}|= bh; = 2 \\cdot 2; = 4$.\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [2,4]$. The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n$A_{2}|= \\frac{1}{2}bh; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2; = 2$.\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n$\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x =-A_2 = -2$.\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{4}f(x)dx &= A_{1} - A_{2} \\\\\n&= 4 - 2 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078232 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "189d4a9a-fb2c-57b2-b9f6-9bec14fa1062" :question/math-academy-id "e-434" :question/problem "Given the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-4}^4 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/626/Source/Images/eb1ab74ae544a1b33f0f6c89141309e6.png)" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of four parts: the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,0]$, a line segment for $x\\in (0,2)$, a line segment for $[MATH: x\\in (2,4],]$ and the point at $x=2$. So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-4}^{4}f(x)dx &= ∫_{-4}^{0}f(x)dx + ∫_{0}^{2}f(x)dx + ∫_{2}^{2}f(x)dx + ∫_{2}^{4}f(x)dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{-4}^{0}f(x)dx_(⏟)_(A_{1}) + ∫_{0}^{2}f(x)dx_(⏟)_(-A_{2}) + 0 + ∫_{2}^{4}f(x)dx_(⏟)_(A_{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [-4,0]$. The corresponding region is a semi-circle with a radius of $2$, so we find the area using the formula\n$A_{1}|= \\frac{1}{2}πr^{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot π \\cdot 2^{2}; = 2π$.\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in (0,2)$. The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n$A_{2}|= \\frac{1}{2}bh; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2; = 2$.\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n$\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = -A_2 = -2$.\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $[MATH: x \\in (2,4].]$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n$A_{3}|= \\frac{1}{2}bh; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2; = 2$.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-4}^{4}f(x)dx &= A_{1} - A_{2} + A_{3} \\\\\n&= 2π - 2 + 2 \\\\\n&= 2π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6269ba2-e024-5bc9-ac37-575cbdbde85b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π + \\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075276} {:answer/id #uuid "ceab8144-3f0e-54d6-bf88-30c117547bba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2} - \\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075277} {:answer/id #uuid "12e8e10f-9759-56e8-bd86-24af1cdd7660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2} + \\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075278} {:answer/id #uuid "6ae61959-ef88-56d8-abdf-df45076d66e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π - \\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075279} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd65bec-3e36-5823-aafd-b9a9649c72fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2} - \\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ceab8144-3f0e-54d6-bf88-30c117547bba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2} - \\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #e